WEBVTT

NOTE Paragraph boundaries measured from synthesis; timing within paragraphs is approximate.

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.186
Infrastructure assessment examines
how identity, network access,

00:00:04.186 --> 00:00:08.334
workload configuration, and software
dependencies combine in deployment.

00:00:08.334 --> 00:00:11.905
Inputs include cloud policies,
infrastructure-as-code changes,

00:00:11.905 --> 00:00:16.743
container image inventories, orchestration
manifests, and observed runtime settings.

00:00:16.743 --> 00:00:18.932
AI can compare these large collections

00:00:18.932 --> 00:00:23.540
and organize candidate findings around
affected resources and intended controls.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:27.679
For identity and access management,
examine the principal, allowed action,

00:00:27.679 --> 00:00:31.706
resource scope, and applicable
conditions. A policy that permits writing

00:00:31.706 --> 00:00:35.733
many storage resources may exceed
a workload's need to read one dataset.

00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:39.368
A model can highlight the mismatch
and draft a narrower proposal.

00:00:39.368 --> 00:00:42.612
Confirm effective permissions
and application requirements

00:00:42.612 --> 00:00:45.632
before recommending a change.
Valid policy syntax says

00:00:45.632 --> 00:00:48.820
little about whether the resulting
access is appropriate.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.168
Container assessment adds runtime context.

00:00:51.168 --> 00:00:55.361
Image package findings do not describe
workload permissions or host access.

00:00:55.361 --> 00:00:58.772
A deployment that combines
elevated privileges with sensitive

00:00:58.772 --> 00:01:01.847
host mounts deserves examination
beyond the image scan.

00:01:01.847 --> 00:01:04.586
Check whether the settings
are actually deployed,

00:01:04.586 --> 00:01:08.500
what resources they expose, and which
isolation controls are enforced.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:13.140
A claimed compensating control is useful
only if its relevant behavior is verified.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.855
Comparing intended configuration
with deployed state can expose drift,

00:01:16.855 --> 00:01:19.933
including access-rule changes
and altered secret delivery.

00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:23.594
Models can summarize differences
and connect related cloud resources,

00:01:23.594 --> 00:01:27.203
but should not receive raw secrets
merely to explain their handling.

00:01:27.203 --> 00:01:31.077
The practical output is a contextual
finding with configuration evidence,

00:01:31.077 --> 00:01:34.367
affected scope, and a remediation
proposal that can be tested.

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:37.233
Cloud assessment and container
security offer distinct

00:01:37.233 --> 00:01:39.780
follow-up paths within
this infrastructure work.
